 "n_TF_in_panel": 4,
 "mean_regulon_hits_of10_for_TFs": 0.0,
 "verdict": "FULL STACK on 60 held-out knockouts at honest deployment -- and it REFINES my own over-correction from before. (1) MAGNITUDE: predicted response size correlates with actual at Spearman 0.459 -- a usable order-of-magnitude 'how disruptive is this knockout'. (2) IDENTITY -- and here is the nuance I got wrong last turn: the top-10 deployment precision SCALES WITH KNOCKOUT STRENGTH. STRONG knockouts (>=50 genes move) get 9.0/10 in the top-10 on REAL full-universe deployment (RPL27 10/10, RBM25 10/10, EIF2S3 9/10) -- so the '9/10' is REAL, not a pure subsampling artifact; but WEAK knockouts get only 0.81/10, and the average is 2.02/10. The subsampling in the earlier eval inflated the AVERAGE to look uniformly 9/10; the truth is 9/10 for STRONG knockouts, ~1/10 for weak, ~2/10 on average -- and in all cases the hits are the GENERIC usual-suspect genes (a strong knockout moves so much of the transcriptome that predicting the usual movers lands), not protein-specific. (3) NEAR-FIELD: only 4 panel KOs are curated TFs and their regulon top-10 got 0.0/10 (too small a sample to conclude). BOTTOM LINE, measured at scale: the stack tells you HOW BIG the response is (Spearman 0.459), and WHICH genes with a precision that scales from ~9/10 for strong knockouts down to ~1/10 for weak ones -- but always the generic stress genes, never protein-unique. That is the honest, complete deployment picture, and it corrects both the inflated 9/10 AND my too-harsh 'it's just 1/10' retraction.",
 "note": "Full-stack run on 60 held-out knockouts at honest deployment (train on rest, score every gene). MAGNITUDE Spearman 0.459 (usable order-of-magnitude). IDENTITY: top-10 deployment precision SCALES with knockout strength -- STRONG KOs (>=50 movers) get 9.0/10, WEAK (<15) 0.81/10, average 2.02/10. So the '9/10' is REAL for strong knockouts (not a pure subsampling artifact), ~1/10 for weak; the subsampling had inflated the AVERAGE to look uniformly 9/10; and in all cases the hits are generic usual-suspect genes, not protein-specific. NEAR-FIELD regulon top-10 0.0/10 for the 4 TF panel KOs (small sample). Corrects both the inflated 9/10 and the too-harsh 1/10 retraction: the honest deployment number scales from ~9/10 (strong) to ~1/10 (weak), always generic."
}